36 requests (30 distinct; the rest are repeats).

                                  cost  calls  out tok     p50   correct
  baseline                     0.00495     36    3,875    1.4s    28/36  
  + ask for one sentence       0.00277     36      265    0.9s    31/36  
  + cap the output             0.00276     36      255    0.9s    31/36  
  + trim the context to 4      0.00160     36      264    0.9s    29/36  
  + exact cache                0.00134     30      224    0.9s    29/36  

  cost      73% lower
  quality   28/36 -> 29/36  (+3%)
  latency   1.4s -> 0.9s median

  (placeholder rates; the ratios are real, the dollars are illustrative)

Both numbers, on the same line, every time. A cheaper system that is quietly
worse is not an optimisation — it is a regression with a business case, and
the only thing that tells them apart is the right-hand column.

Read the steps in order of what they bought.

Asking for one sentence is the largest single win here and the least
fashionable. Output tokens are a minority of the volume at several times the
price, and the default behaviour of every model is to explain itself.

Capping max_tokens is not the same lever. It truncates rather than shortens,
which produces a cut-off answer at full price — §27.4's stop reason is how you
find out that is happening. It is a safety rail, not an optimisation.

Trimming the context is the one to watch. Fewer passages is less input and
fewer chances to retrieve the right one, so it trades cost against §22.6's
context recall directly. Measure both or you will trade away the answer.

And the cache is free money in proportion to how much your traffic repeats,
which is a property of your users rather than your code. Measure the repeat
rate before building anything.
